 Growing My Skills in IT Support & Customer Service

Recently, I started learning about Customer Service in IT Support through Cisco Networking Academy, and I’ve been gaining a better understanding of how professional help desk and support teams operate.

One thing I’ve learned is that IT support goes beyond simply fixing technical problems. It involves properly managing customer requests, prioritizing issues, communicating effectively, and ensuring that problems are resolved within agreed service standards.

Here are some of the key areas I’ve been learning:

 Help Desk Ticket Workflow
I learned how a customer issue moves through the support process — from the moment a ticket is created, to categorizing and prioritizing the issue, assigning it to the appropriate support personnel, resolving it, and finally closing the ticket.

 Queue Management
Queue management involves organizing and prioritizing multiple customer requests so that urgent and important issues receive appropriate attention while other requests are handled efficiently.

 SLAs (Service Level Agreements)
I learned that SLAs define the expected level of service, including how quickly a support team should respond to and resolve certain types of issues. Understanding SLAs helps support teams manage customer expectations and maintain service quality.

 KPIs (Key Performance Indicators)
KPIs are measurable indicators used to evaluate the performance of a support team. Examples include response time, resolution time, ticket volume, customer satisfaction, and first-contact resolution.

Managing Multiple Tickets Simultaneously
IT support professionals may need to handle several customer issues at the same time. I’m learning the importance of prioritization, organization, time management, and keeping accurate ticket information while working on multiple requests.

 AI Tools in Ticketing Systems
I also learned about the growing role of AI in help desk environments. AI can assist with things such as categorizing and prioritizing tickets, suggesting solutions, automating repetitive tasks, and helping support agents find relevant information faster.

This learning experience is helping me understand that excellent IT support requires both technical knowledge and strong customer service skills.

I’m continuing to develop myself in IT Support, Customer Service, Networking, CRM and Help Desk technologies, and I’m looking forward to putting what I’m learning into practical projects.

 Learning. Practicing. Improving. One skill at a time.

